    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-29989",
    ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{3/5}}$ and that $F^{\u2032}(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x)$."
  },
  {
    ":db/id": 17592186233179,
    ":question/difficulty": {
      ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
    ":question/id": "25edeff2-faa4-50c4-bbfd-3291d7ac7694",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-113705",
    ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}={x}^{-4/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/3}}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}+C \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{1/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
  {
    ":db/id": 17592186233751,
    ":question/difficulty": {
      ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
    },
    ":question/id": "6a192223-274d-5d58-847a-c92d2faa98df",
    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-29633",
    ":question/problem": "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}?$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Note that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{-4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
  {
    ":db/id": 17592186233752,
    ":question/difficulty": {
      ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
