    ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if $F'(x) = f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{\u2032}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{\u2032}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}= x^{-3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  }
